#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Set up backend and coupling map {#set-up-backend-and-coupling-map}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "First, select a backend\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Then obtain its coupling map\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In order to benchmark as many two-qubit gates as possible simultaneously we "
"separate the coupling map into a `layered_coupling_map`. This object "
"contains a list of layers where each layer is a list of edges on which two-"
"qubit gates can be executed at the same time. This is also called an edge "
"coloring of the coupling map.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Characterization experiments {#characterization-experiments}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A series of experiments is used to characterize the main properties of the "
"qubits in a QPU. These are $T_1$, $T_2$, readout error, and single-qubit and"
" two-qubit gate error. We'll briefly summarize what these properties are and"
" refer to experiments in the [`qiskit-experiments`](https://qiskit-"
"community.github.io/qiskit-experiments/index.html) package that are used to "
"characterize them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "T1 {#t1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$T_1$ is the characteristic time it takes for an excited qubit to fall to the ground state due to amplitude-damping decoherence processes. In a [$T_1$ experiment](https://qiskit-community.github.io/qiskit-experiments/manuals/characterization/t1.html), we measure an excited qubit after a delay. The larger the delay time is, the more\n"
"likely is the qubit to fall to the ground state. The goal of the\n"
"experiment is to characterize the decay rate of the qubit towards the\n"
"ground state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "T2 {#t2}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$T_2$ represents the amount of time required for a single qubit's Bloch\n"
"vector projection on the XY plane to fall to approximately 37% ($\\frac{1}{e}$) of\n"
"its initial amplitude due to dephasing decoherence processes. In a [$T_2$ Hahn Echo experiment](https://qiskit-community.github.io/qiskit-experiments/manuals/characterization/t2hahn.html), we can estimate the rate of this decay.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"State preparation and measurement (SPAM) error characterization {#state-"
"preparation-and-measurement-spam-error-characterization}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In a [SPAM-error characterization experiment](https://qiskit-"
"community.github.io/qiskit-"
"experiments/manuals/measurement/readout_mitigation.html) qubit are prepared "
"in a certain state ($\\vert 0 \\rangle$ or $\\vert 1 \\rangle$) and "
"measured. The probability of measuring a state different than the one "
"prepared then gives the probability of the error.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Single-qubit and two-qubit randomized benchmarking {#single-qubit-and-two-"
"qubit-randomized-benchmarking}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[Randomized benchmarking (RB)](https://qiskit-community.github.io/qiskit-experiments/manuals/verification/randomized_benchmarking.html) is a popular protocol for characterizing the error rate of\n"
"quantum processors. An RB experiment consists of the generation of random Clifford\n"
"circuits on the given qubits such that the unitary computed by the circuits is the\n"
"identity. After running the circuits, the number of shots resulting in an error (that is, an output different from the ground state) are counted, and from this data one can infer error estimates for the quantum device, by calculating the Error Per Clifford.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "QPU properties over time {#qpu-properties-over-time}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Looking at the reported QPU properties over time (we'll consider a single "
"week below), we see how these can fluctuate on a scale of a single day. "
"Small fluctuations can happen even within a day. In this scenario, the "
"reported properties (updated once per day) will not accurately capture the "
"current status of the QPU. Moreover, if a job is transpiled locally (using "
"current reported properties) and submitted but executed only at a later time"
" (minutes or days), it may run the risk of having used outdated properties "
"for qubit selection in the transpilation step. This highlights the "
"importance of having updated information about the QPU at execution time. "
"First, let's retrieve the properties over a certain time range.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Then, let's plot the values\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/real-time-"
"benchmarking-for-qubit-selection/extracted-"
"outputs/e0ba509d-e0e0-438b-aedf-5e01919c7d4f-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You can see that over several days some of the qubit properties can change "
"considerably. This highlights the importance of having fresh information of "
"the QPU status, to be able to select the best performing qubits for an "
"experiment.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 2: Optimize problem for quantum hardware execution {#step-2-optimize-"
"problem-for-quantum-hardware-execution}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "No optimization of the circuits or operators is done in this tutorial.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 3: Execute using Qiskit primitives {#step-3-execute-using-qiskit-"
"primitives}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Execute a quantum circuit with default qubit selection {#execute-a-quantum-"
"circuit-with-default-qubit-selection}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As a reference result of performance, we'll execute a quantum circuit on a "
"QPU by using the default qubits, which are the qubits selected with the "
"requested backend properties. We will use `optimization_level = 3`.  This "
"setting includes the most advanced transpilation optimization, and uses "
"target properties (like operation errors) to select the best performing "
"qubits for execution.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Execute a quantum circuit with real-time qubit selection {#execute-a-"
"quantum-circuit-with-real-time-qubit-selection}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this section, we'll investigate the importance of having updated "
"information on qubit properties of the QPU for optimal results. First, we'll"
" carry out a full suite of QPU characterization experiments ($T_1$, $T_2$, "
"SPAM, single-qubit RB and two-qubit RB), which we can then use to update the"
" backend properties. This allows the pass manager to select qubits for the "
"execution based on fresh information about the QPU, possibly improving "
"execution performances. Second, we execute the Bell pair circuit and we "
"compare the fidelity obtained after selecting the qubits with update QPU "
"properties to the fidelity we obtained before when we use the default "
"reported properties for qubit selection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"caution\">\n"
"Note that some of the characterization experiments might fail when the fitting routine cannot fit a curve to the measured data. If you see warnings coming from these experiments, inspect them to understand which characterization failed on which qubits, and try adjusting the parameters of the experiment (like the times for $T_1$, $T_2$, or the number lengths of RB experiments).\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 4: Post-process and return result in desired classical format "
"{#step-4-post-process-and-return-result-in-desired-classical-format}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Finally, let's compare the fidelity of the Bell state obtained in the two "
"different settings:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`original`, that is with the default qubits chosen by the transpiler based "
"on reported properties of the backend.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`updated`, that is with the qubits chosen based on updated properties of the"
" backend after characterization experiments have run.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/real-time-"
"benchmarking-for-qubit-selection/extracted-"
"outputs/656ec97a-3fd9-4635-9a98-1c5589761689-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Not all runs will show improvement in performance due to the real-time characterization - and with increasing chain length, and thus less freedom to choose physical qubits, the importance of updated device information becomes less substantial. However, it is good practice to collect fresh data on the device properties to understand its performance. Occasionally, transient two-level systems may affect the performance of some of the qubits. Real-time data can inform us when such events are happening and help us avoid experimental failures in such instances.\n"
"<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"Call to action\">\n"
"Try to apply this method to your executions and determine how much of a benefit you get! You can also try and see how much improvements you get from different backends.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

